  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal Express recorded $75.6M in contract obligations in FY2027 to date. Federal Express won 151 awards this fiscal year so far, with an average award size of $501K. Obligations fell 80.5% year over year. Federal Express received most FY2027 to date obligations from the Department of Veterans Affairs, which obligated $75.1M. Federal Express supplied Couriers and Express Delivery Services to the government, which totaled $75.6M in FY2027 to date. Federal Express obligations in earlier years were larger: FY22 $309M, FY23 $275M, FY24 $677M, FY25 $582M, and FY26 $388M. Other FY2027 buyers include the Department of Justice $179K, NASA $121K, and the Department of the Treasury $83.3K. Federal Express had smaller FY2027 obligations with the Department of Transportation $40.8K, Department of Commerce $30K, and Department of Energy $10K. Federal Express recorded minimal obligations with the Federal Maritime Commission $4K, Court Services and Offender Supervision Agency $2.5K, and Department of Homeland Security $1K. The FY2027 totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did Federal Express obligations fall 80.5% year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 80.5% year over year. Federal Express obligations in FY27 are $75.6M, which is lower than FY26 obligations of $388M and earlier peaks such as FY24 $677M. The Department of Veterans Affairs provided $75.1M of FY2027 obligations, while other agencies contributed much smaller amounts. The FY2027 totals are fiscal year to date and partial."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Federal Express's top buyers and what markets do they buy in?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the top buyer with $75.1M in FY2027 to date. Other FY2027 buyers include the Department of Justice $179K, NASA $121K, and the Department of the Treasury $83.3K, followed by smaller obligations from the Department of Transportation $40.8K and Department of Commerce $30K. Couriers and Express Delivery Services is the largest NAICS market at $75.6M, with General Freight Trucking, Long-Distance, Less Than Truckload at $600."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Federal Express make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Federal Express has $75.6M in contract obligations in FY2027 to date. Federal Express won 151 awards this fiscal year to date, with an average award size of $501K. Obligations fell 80.5% year over year. Historical annual obligations include FY22 $309M, FY23 $275M, FY24 $677M, FY25 $582M, and FY26 $388M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Federal Express?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, with $75.1M in FY2027 to date. Other FY2027 buyers are the Department of Justice $179K, National Aeronautics and Space Administration $121K, Department of the Treasury $83.3K, Department of Transportation $40.8K, Department of Commerce $30K, Department of Energy $10K, Federal Maritime Commission $4K, Court Services and Offender Supervision Agency $2.5K, and Department of Homeland Security $1K."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Federal Express sell to the government?",
